.TH PACKSRC 1
.SH NAME
packsrc \- pack a tree into tar files, or a manifest into disks
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B packsrc tar
tree outdir budget
.br
.B packsrc part
manifest outdir blocks inodes
.SH DESCRIPTION
.I packsrc
sorts a set of files into volumes under a size budget.  It has two
modes, because a distribution has two kinds of payload.
.PP
.B packsrc tar
walks
.I tree
and writes a series of tar files into
.IR outdir ,
each one as close to
.I budget
512 byte blocks as the tree allows, with the members of each written to
.BR list. N
and the archive itself to
.BR src.tar. N ,
numbered from 1.  This is for the text-heavy parts of a distribution,
which compress and which are cheaper to write as one member that is a
tar than as a thousand members that are files.
.PP
The unit of packing is one directory's own files, and a directory with
no subdirectories is never split.  Units are taken in sorted order and
accumulated into one tar until it would go over budget, at which point
the last unit is backed off and starts the next tar.  One pass, and no
search: a candidate's size is computed from the file sizes rather than
found by building it.
.PP
A unit that is over budget on its own goes into a tar by itself rather
than being cut in half, and the tar it lands in is reported as over.
.PP
.B packsrc part
reads
.IR manifest ,
a list of "size path" lines with the path relative to the image's
root, and writes
.BR list. N
into
.IR outdir
for each volume, one path to a line.  The output is a partition: every
path of the manifest appears exactly once, on one disk.
.PP
.I blocks
is the volume's free blocks, two below the filesystem size less the
inodes \- the boot area is in front of the filesystem and is not
counted either.  A file costs what
.I bmap
charges, which is not its length in blocks: eight addresses in the
inode are direct, the ninth is one indirect block, and past 1792 blocks
a second level is added one block per 256.
.PP
.I inodes
limits the volume too, and it is not a formality.  A directory costs an
inode and costs blocks, 32 entries to a block at 16 bytes an entry, so
224 names in one directory is seven blocks that hold no file data.
Every directory above a file has to exist for the file to land, and a
directory's parent grows by a block each time the parent's own entry
count crosses a multiple of 32 \- which a disk holding one file from
.B /usr/src/cmd
pays for, having to build
.BR /usr ,
.B /usr/src
and
.B /usr/src/cmd
to hold it.
.PP
The unit is again one directory's own files, and a unit again goes on
one disk whole.  A unit that will not fit on an empty disk goes a file
at a time instead, because on the five inch media most units will not:
.B /bin
is 4200 blocks against a budget of 727.
.PP
The partition is printed as it is made, a line per disk with the blocks
and inodes used and the percentage of the budget, and a total.  A disk
that comes out over 100 per cent is reported rather than refused: it is
the caller's to judge, and the caller's
.IR icheck
that says whether the forecast held.
.SH "SEE ALSO"
tar(1), mkfs(1)
